WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:08.191
Fieldnote zero. Three parts.

2
00:00:09.454 --> 00:00:11.864
Azure SPI connects three things:

3
00:00:11.934 --> 00:00:17.134
the stack that runs OSDU, the
Azure provider inside each service,

4
00:00:17.134 --> 00:00:20.104
and the workflows that keep
that provider current.

5
00:00:20.254 --> 00:00:23.054
This site explains how
those parts fit together,

6
00:00:23.054 --> 00:00:27.464
from creating an environment to
building and testing a service change.

7
00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.180
Start inside one service.

8
00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:35.540
Shared OSDU code handles the request.

9
00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:39.540
Where it needs a database, a
queue, or a storage account,

10
00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:42.820
it calls a provider through
the Service Provider Interface,

11
00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:49.060
SPI. The provider implements the
cloud-specific behavior behind that interface,

12
00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:52.980
and the Azure provider ships in
the same image as the shared code,

13
00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:55.620
with no network hop between them.

14
00:00:57.918 --> 00:01:01.878
That provider is Microsoft's to maintain,
and the community plans to remove the

15
00:01:01.878 --> 00:01:03.928
Azure code from its repositories.

16
00:01:04.398 --> 00:01:07.758
So each OSDU service gets
a public service fork,

17
00:01:07.758 --> 00:01:11.278
one repository per service,
that owns its Azure provider,

18
00:01:11.278 --> 00:01:14.248
its Azure tests, and
its build configuration.

19
00:01:14.798 --> 00:01:19.038
Every day a workflow generates a branch
from the upstream tip with the whole

20
00:01:19.038 --> 00:01:23.998
provider tree absent, Azure included, and
the fork's own provider joins it on the

21
00:01:23.998 --> 00:01:25.368
integration branch.

22
00:01:25.998 --> 00:01:29.448
Upstream's planned removal
deletes nothing on the fork side.

23
00:01:31.794 --> 00:01:34.594
A change in the fork
becomes a candidate image,

24
00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:36.284
addressed by digest.

25
00:01:36.674 --> 00:01:40.204
A passing build is not evidence
that the provider works on Azure.

26
00:01:40.514 --> 00:01:44.604
The template includes a workflow for
testing a candidate in a running stack:

27
00:01:44.914 --> 00:01:48.124
it writes the digest into
the environment's image lock,

28
00:01:48.194 --> 00:01:53.164
waits until the pod runs that digest,
runs the suites the service declares,

29
00:01:53.234 --> 00:01:57.644
and writes the captured canonical
image back while it still owns the pin;

30
00:01:58.034 --> 00:02:00.844
a lost run can leave
that work for a person.

31
00:02:01.394 --> 00:02:03.804
The partition fork
adopted it in September,

32
00:02:03.874 --> 00:02:08.124
and a run on its main branch has
passed both of the suites it declares.

33
00:02:10.574 --> 00:02:15.064
The stack itself is a development and
test environment in one resource group:

34
00:02:15.294 --> 00:02:19.294
AKS beside Cosmos DB,
Storage, Service Bus,

35
00:02:19.294 --> 00:02:24.294
and Key Vault. spi up creates the
Azure resources and starts Flux,

36
00:02:24.294 --> 00:02:27.784
which continues assembling
workloads after the command returns.

37
00:02:27.934 --> 00:02:31.144
It is shared and disposable,
and it is not production.

38
00:02:31.534 --> 00:02:33.454
Two repositories carry this:

39
00:02:33.454 --> 00:02:39.294
osdu-spi-stack builds the
environment, and osdu-spi

40
00:02:39.294 --> 00:02:41.784
supplies every fork's workflows.

41
00:02:41.854 --> 00:02:44.584
After the lessons you can
locate a change in a fork,

42
00:02:44.654 --> 00:02:48.584
follow it into an image, and say what
a test in the running stack proves.

43
00:02:48.734 --> 00:02:50.664
The lessons follow one example:

44
00:02:50.894 --> 00:02:54.174
a partition lookup for
opendes, through the stack,

45
00:02:54.174 --> 00:02:56.744
into the provider, and around the fork.

46
00:02:59.262 --> 00:03:00.792
Start with lesson one:

47
00:03:01.022 --> 00:03:02.392
anatomy of a stack.

